1
00:00:00,566 --> 00:00:02,233
You are on that subway again

2
00:00:02,333 --> 00:00:03,333
The doors close

3
00:00:03,600 --> 00:00:06,033
The same expression you wear every day appears in the glass

4
00:00:06,400 --> 00:00:08,333
You glance down at the calendar on your phone

5
00:00:08,400 --> 00:00:09,466
You pause

6
00:00:09,733 --> 00:00:11,533
How does half a year feel like last month

7
00:00:11,733 --> 00:00:15,733
Many people over thirty know this hard to explain feeling

8
00:00:16,066 --> 00:00:18,266
The Spring Festival just ended and National Day is almost here again

9
00:00:18,566 --> 00:00:21,133
The school uniform you just bought for your child already seems too short

10
00:00:21,466 --> 00:00:23,800
The plan you made last year is still in your notes

11
00:00:23,933 --> 00:00:25,733
Yet the dates have already turned a whole page

12
00:00:26,066 --> 00:00:28,533
We easily blame this on age

13
00:00:28,700 --> 00:00:31,000
We say time naturally speeds up as people get older

14
00:00:31,166 --> 00:00:32,366
That is not entirely wrong

15
00:00:32,533 --> 00:00:34,000
But it only describes the surface

16
00:00:34,300 --> 00:00:35,666
The more painful part is this

17
00:00:35,866 --> 00:00:37,533
Time has not suddenly sped up

18
00:00:37,700 --> 00:00:40,366
Too many days have grown too similar in your mind

19
00:00:40,666 --> 00:00:41,566
The same road

20
00:00:41,566 --> 00:00:42,600
The same desk

21
00:00:42,733 --> 00:00:44,133
The same delivery app page

22
00:00:44,333 --> 00:00:46,200
The same phone scrolling after dinner

23
00:00:46,466 --> 00:00:47,866
It is not that nothing happened

24
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:50,666
It is just hard to remember each event on its own afterward

25
00:00:50,933 --> 00:00:55,366
Looking back on a week Monday through Friday feels like one sheet copied five times

26
00:00:55,533 --> 00:00:59,033
Looking back on a year you are left with a few holidays and photos

27
00:00:59,266 --> 00:01:03,033
The real afternoons you lived through seem compressed into one folder

28
00:01:03,333 --> 00:01:04,666
This does not mean your memory is bad

29
00:01:04,866 --> 00:01:06,766
This is simply what the brain naturally does

30
00:01:06,933 --> 00:01:10,600
It cannot save every second of your morning commute exactly as it happened

31
00:01:10,766 --> 00:01:12,466
That would fill your entire mind

32
00:01:12,700 --> 00:01:14,866
So it merges similar experiences

33
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:16,400
Just like organizing a closet

34
00:01:16,533 --> 00:01:18,266
You fold the same clothes into one pile

35
00:01:18,400 --> 00:01:20,400
Different days are folded into one category too

36
00:01:20,566 --> 00:01:23,466
Only especially fresh experiences get hung up separately

37
00:01:23,600 --> 00:01:24,933
The first place you ever visit

38
00:01:24,966 --> 00:01:26,400
A sudden downpour

39
00:01:26,566 --> 00:01:29,066
A night when you had a serious conversation with someone until very late

40
00:01:29,166 --> 00:01:32,766
And that afternoon when you gathered the courage to do something you once feared

41
00:01:32,866 --> 00:01:35,266
They leave a small pin in your memory

42
00:01:35,400 --> 00:01:38,333
Later one thought can pull that stretch of time back out

43
00:01:38,533 --> 00:01:40,600
So time does not become faster out of nowhere

44
00:01:40,766 --> 00:01:42,833
It has simply grown thinner in your memory

45
00:01:43,100 --> 00:01:45,533
If a year has recently felt like a month

46
00:01:45,600 --> 00:01:47,233
It does not necessarily mean you are living too fast

47
00:01:47,400 --> 00:01:50,866
You may just be turning too many days into one borderless day

48
00:01:51,166 --> 00:01:52,766
That sounds a little sad

49
00:01:52,866 --> 00:01:55,533
But it gives us a very practical direction

50
00:01:55,733 --> 00:01:57,766
Do not rush to pack your schedule even tighter

51
00:01:57,866 --> 00:02:00,366
You may not need more things to do

52
00:02:00,400 --> 00:02:02,733
You may need a few moments your brain can remember

53
00:02:03,200 --> 00:02:04,933
Have you ever scrolled through your photo gallery

54
00:02:05,333 --> 00:02:07,133
There are plenty of photos from last summer

55
00:02:07,400 --> 00:02:10,466
But what you truly remember is rarely an ordinary Tuesday

56
00:02:10,533 --> 00:02:12,833
It is the sunburn on your shoulders from that day by the sea

57
00:02:13,100 --> 00:02:15,833
The pain when a box cut your finger during a move

58
00:02:16,100 --> 00:02:18,533
The sudden phone call after dinner

59
00:02:18,866 --> 00:02:20,733
Memory is rarely like surveillance footage

60
00:02:20,933 --> 00:02:22,133
It is more like editing

61
00:02:22,300 --> 00:02:24,766
The brain cuts away countless similar images

62
00:02:24,866 --> 00:02:26,733
And keeps the moments with change

63
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:29,200
Psychology has a simple observation

64
00:02:29,400 --> 00:02:32,400
Fresh experiences often make time feel longer

65
00:02:32,600 --> 00:02:34,166
Not because the clock slows down

66
00:02:34,166 --> 00:02:37,333
But because you take in more details during that time

67
00:02:37,533 --> 00:02:38,933
When you first learned to drive

68
00:02:39,000 --> 00:02:40,633
The pressure of your right foot on the accelerator

69
00:02:40,733 --> 00:02:41,933
The cars in the rearview mirror

70
00:02:42,100 --> 00:02:43,966
The sudden rise in your instructors voice

71
00:02:44,100 --> 00:02:45,966
You notice every detail

72
00:02:46,100 --> 00:02:47,733
That hour passes very slowly

73
00:02:47,800 --> 00:02:49,166
But once you become familiar with it

74
00:02:49,166 --> 00:02:51,666
Your body almost drives the same route by itself

75
00:02:51,900 --> 00:02:54,866
When you get home you may not even remember how many red lights you stopped at

76
00:02:55,166 --> 00:02:56,733
Familiarity makes life easier

77
00:02:56,800 --> 00:02:59,033
But it also makes memories thinner

78
00:02:59,200 --> 00:03:00,733
This is the double edge of repetition

79
00:03:00,766 --> 00:03:03,266
It saves you from relearning everything

80
00:03:03,366 --> 00:03:06,033
But it also makes many days lose their own names

81
00:03:06,166 --> 00:03:07,933
There is another more hidden reason

82
00:03:08,066 --> 00:03:10,766
We are now too used to filling every gap with our phones

83
00:03:10,933 --> 00:03:12,400
Ten seconds of scrolling while waiting for the elevator

84
00:03:12,533 --> 00:03:13,933
Five minutes of scrolling while eating

85
00:03:14,066 --> 00:03:15,966
One more look at your messages before a shower

86
00:03:16,066 --> 00:03:17,833
These fragments look tiny

87
00:03:17,933 --> 00:03:21,733
But they fill every place that could have offered a pause with someone elses content

88
00:03:21,800 --> 00:03:23,766
You take in lots of information every day

89
00:03:23,800 --> 00:03:26,533
Yet you have little time to connect your own experiences

90
00:03:26,600 --> 00:03:29,066
Just as your brain starts to look back on the day

91
00:03:29,200 --> 00:03:30,833
The next video arrives

92
00:03:30,966 --> 00:03:32,733
Just as you are about to figure out a thought

93
00:03:32,766 --> 00:03:34,233
Another group message pops up

94
00:03:34,366 --> 00:03:36,066
In the end you did not lack experiences

95
00:03:36,133 --> 00:03:38,866
You failed to turn them into memories that belong to you

96
00:03:39,066 --> 00:03:42,033
Many people think time speeds up because life is too busy

97
00:03:42,200 --> 00:03:43,533
But that is not the whole story

98
00:03:43,700 --> 00:03:47,666
Busy people who solve different problems each day may remember more

99
00:03:47,800 --> 00:03:49,733
What really makes a year disappear easily

100
00:03:49,733 --> 00:03:51,733
Is repetition combined with autoplay

101
00:03:51,900 --> 00:03:53,400
Repetition merges your days

102
00:03:53,500 --> 00:03:55,600
Autoplay leaves no time to look back

103
00:03:55,733 --> 00:03:57,366
So twelve months pass

104
00:03:57,400 --> 00:03:59,533
And you only remember that you seemed busy the whole time

105
00:03:59,600 --> 00:04:01,333
Yet you cannot say what all that busyness produced

106
00:04:01,466 --> 00:04:03,400
Repetition does not steal your time

107
00:04:03,600 --> 00:04:05,733
It makes time lose its boundaries

108
00:04:06,100 --> 00:04:07,800
Let us start with a very common evening

109
00:04:07,800 --> 00:04:08,933
You get home from work

110
00:04:08,933 --> 00:04:09,666
You take off your shoes

111
00:04:09,666 --> 00:04:10,466
You set down the takeout

112
00:04:10,466 --> 00:04:11,900
And your phone is already glowing in your hand

113
00:04:11,900 --> 00:04:13,866
You only meant to rest for ten minutes

114
00:04:13,866 --> 00:04:15,800
But when you look up it is almost one in the morning

115
00:04:15,800 --> 00:04:17,600
This evening was not completely empty either

116
00:04:17,600 --> 00:04:19,100
You watched many people travel

117
00:04:19,100 --> 00:04:20,166
Heard many opinions

118
00:04:20,166 --> 00:04:21,400
And even remembered a few jokes

119
00:04:21,400 --> 00:04:23,533
But most of it did not belong to your life

120
00:04:23,533 --> 00:04:25,600
By the next morning it has all scattered away

121
00:04:25,600 --> 00:04:28,333
The only thing left is the feeling that you did nothing again last night

122
00:04:28,333 --> 00:04:29,166
Now think about the weekend

123
00:04:29,166 --> 00:04:30,333
You have been exhausted all week

124
00:04:30,333 --> 00:04:31,466
Finally you do not have to clock in

125
00:04:31,466 --> 00:04:33,900
So you tell yourself you will not plan anything today

126
00:04:33,900 --> 00:04:35,133
There is nothing wrong with that

127
00:04:35,166 --> 00:04:36,733
Of course you can do nothing

128
00:04:36,733 --> 00:04:41,700
The problem is that the platform ends up choosing a little bit of everything for you

129
00:04:41,700 --> 00:04:42,666
One show in the morning

130
00:04:42,666 --> 00:04:44,000
Several rounds of short videos in the afternoon

131
00:04:44,100 --> 00:04:45,933
And the same familiar takeout again at night

132
00:04:45,933 --> 00:04:48,333
When the weekend ends your body may feel a little looser

133
00:04:48,333 --> 00:04:50,266
But inside you do not really feel filled up

134
00:04:50,266 --> 00:04:52,366
Because you never get the full feeling of rest

135
00:04:52,366 --> 00:04:54,866
You were simply covered over by content told in a flat stream

136
00:04:54,866 --> 00:04:56,600
Then there is a sentence parents often say

137
00:04:56,600 --> 00:04:58,366
How did our child get so big so fast

138
00:04:58,366 --> 00:05:00,366
Often it is not because they do not love their child

139
00:05:00,366 --> 00:05:03,133
It is precisely because they spend every day driving them around cooking and doing laundry

140
00:05:03,133 --> 00:05:04,700
Those things are simply too similar

141
00:05:04,700 --> 00:05:05,766
Hurry up and get up in the morning

142
00:05:05,766 --> 00:05:06,800
Hurry up and wash up at night

143
00:05:06,800 --> 00:05:11,200
To save effort the brain compresses countless similar moments into a few years of raising a child

144
00:05:11,200 --> 00:05:14,600
But the proof that a child is really growing up hides in the moments that are different

145
00:05:14,600 --> 00:05:15,966
The first time they tie their own shoelaces

146
00:05:15,966 --> 00:05:17,733
The strange question they suddenly ask one day

147
00:05:17,766 --> 00:05:19,766
Getting back up after failing

148
00:05:19,766 --> 00:05:21,433
If you do not stop and notice

149
00:05:21,566 --> 00:05:23,866
These moments will slide past your hands too

150
00:05:23,866 --> 00:05:24,800
Now look at work

151
00:05:24,800 --> 00:05:26,366
What many people fear most is not being busy

152
00:05:26,366 --> 00:05:28,200
It is filling out almost the same forms every week

153
00:05:28,200 --> 00:05:29,266
Holding almost the same meetings

154
00:05:29,266 --> 00:05:30,600
Solving almost the same problems

155
00:05:30,700 --> 00:05:33,566
Watching the calendar move forward while feeling that you have not moved

156
00:05:33,566 --> 00:05:35,766
The danger then is not that you did not work hard

157
00:05:35,766 --> 00:05:37,966
It is that you start mistaking numbness for stability

158
00:05:37,966 --> 00:05:39,266
Stability is certainly good

159
00:05:39,266 --> 00:05:42,800
But if stability means you have no personal description of any day

160
00:05:42,800 --> 00:05:45,366
It slowly becomes an invisible kind of loss

161
00:05:45,366 --> 00:05:48,100
You do not need to travel every day to create memories

162
00:05:48,100 --> 00:05:50,100
Nor do you need to turn every weekend into a task

163
00:05:50,100 --> 00:05:53,033
This is not a video telling you to live more intensely

164
00:05:53,100 --> 00:05:54,400
It only wants to remind you

165
00:05:54,400 --> 00:05:57,200
Your mind will not automatically name your days for you

166
00:05:57,200 --> 00:05:58,533
If you leave no room for difference

167
00:05:58,533 --> 00:06:02,033
Time will quietly merge many important things into the background

168
00:06:02,400 --> 00:06:03,366
So what can you do

169
00:06:03,466 --> 00:06:04,533
The first step is small

170
00:06:04,700 --> 00:06:07,533
Do not rush to ask whether you should change your whole life

171
00:06:07,700 --> 00:06:11,466
Just leave yourself one action each week that differs from your usual routine

172
00:06:11,600 --> 00:06:13,833
You can take a different route home after work

173
00:06:14,000 --> 00:06:16,566
You can have a meal with someone you have not seen in a long time

174
00:06:16,733 --> 00:06:19,600
You can visit a market near home you have never entered

175
00:06:19,700 --> 00:06:21,966
Or you can turn off your headphones and walk for ten minutes

176
00:06:22,166 --> 00:06:23,933
The point is not to do something extraordinary

177
00:06:24,000 --> 00:06:26,800
The point is to make this day a little different from yesterday

178
00:06:27,000 --> 00:06:28,666
Novelty does not have to be expensive

179
00:06:28,700 --> 00:06:30,000
It simply tells your brain

180
00:06:30,066 --> 00:06:31,866
Keep this stretch separate

181
00:06:32,066 --> 00:06:34,166
The second step is to give each experience one sentence

182
00:06:34,266 --> 00:06:35,533
Not to write a long diary

183
00:06:35,533 --> 00:06:38,066
And certainly not to force yourself to keep a polished record of life

184
00:06:38,200 --> 00:06:40,466
Ten seconds before bed is enough for one sentence

185
00:06:40,500 --> 00:06:42,533
What was different about today

186
00:06:42,666 --> 00:06:44,733
When today did I really feel present

187
00:06:44,866 --> 00:06:47,533
Maybe it was one very sweet peach you bought in the afternoon

188
00:06:47,566 --> 00:06:50,466
Maybe you finally spoke your mind in a meeting

189
00:06:50,566 --> 00:06:54,133
Or maybe you put down your phone and finished a meal with your family

190
00:06:54,266 --> 00:06:56,600
That sentence is like putting a label on the day

191
00:06:56,700 --> 00:06:57,933
Look at it again a few months later

192
00:06:57,966 --> 00:07:01,166
And you will find that this stretch of time did not completely disappear

193
00:07:01,200 --> 00:07:03,600
The third step is to leave some room for empty space

194
00:07:03,600 --> 00:07:07,366
Do not hand every minute spent waiting for a ride walking or washing dishes to a screen

195
00:07:07,466 --> 00:07:08,866
This does not mean phones are bad

196
00:07:08,900 --> 00:07:11,666
It means your brain needs a small gap without new input

197
00:07:11,766 --> 00:07:15,133
Only then can it reconnect scattered experiences to your own life

198
00:07:15,133 --> 00:07:18,466
You may start thinking about why that remark during the day made you uncomfortable

199
00:07:18,500 --> 00:07:20,933
You may realize that you actually want to try something

200
00:07:21,000 --> 00:07:24,900
You may also notice that a routine you keep repeating no longer fits who you are now

201
00:07:24,900 --> 00:07:26,966
This is how time grows thicker

202
00:07:26,966 --> 00:07:28,666
Not by stretching the year longer

203
00:07:28,800 --> 00:07:31,933
But by giving the year more places you can find again

204
00:07:31,966 --> 00:07:33,366
One more thing matters

205
00:07:33,466 --> 00:07:36,900
Giving time landmarks does not mean turning every day into a project

206
00:07:36,900 --> 00:07:39,800
Some people hear this and immediately want to buy a planner

207
00:07:39,800 --> 00:07:42,133
They want to make every weekend exciting

208
00:07:42,200 --> 00:07:44,666
But if every new experience becomes a check in

209
00:07:44,733 --> 00:07:46,966
It will soon become another kind of repetition

210
00:07:46,966 --> 00:07:49,933
You do not need to create moments worth posting to your social feed

211
00:07:50,000 --> 00:07:52,233
You only need to reach out from your routine once in a while

212
00:07:52,300 --> 00:07:55,566
And make sure you are living more than a copied week

213
00:07:55,666 --> 00:07:59,066
For example choose one evening each week to eat without any background sound

214
00:07:59,133 --> 00:08:01,600
You will hear chopsticks touching bowls for the first time

215
00:08:01,666 --> 00:08:05,166
And notice that your family is no longer talking about last weeks story

216
00:08:05,200 --> 00:08:08,866
For example buy something you have never bought when you pass the convenience store you always visit

217
00:08:09,000 --> 00:08:10,133
Not for the sake of spending

218
00:08:10,133 --> 00:08:12,966
Just to leave a small side road in a familiar route

219
00:08:12,966 --> 00:08:16,633
For example shrink a thought you keep postponing until you have time into half an hour

220
00:08:16,733 --> 00:08:18,166
Sit in a library for a while

221
00:08:18,166 --> 00:08:19,766
Call an old classmate

222
00:08:19,800 --> 00:08:22,333
Water the plant on the balcony that is almost dying

223
00:08:22,333 --> 00:08:23,933
These things look very small

224
00:08:23,966 --> 00:08:25,233
But they will tell you

225
00:08:25,266 --> 00:08:27,366
This day did not play itself automatically

226
00:08:27,366 --> 00:08:29,333
Part of it was chosen by your own hand

227
00:08:29,333 --> 00:08:31,600
What stays in memory is not always a big event

228
00:08:31,600 --> 00:08:35,566
Often it is the ordinary afternoon when you did not hurry to scroll past it as usual

229
00:08:35,566 --> 00:08:37,400
Many people say they want to slow down

230
00:08:37,400 --> 00:08:39,466
They do not actually want the clock to slow down

231
00:08:39,500 --> 00:08:41,766
They want to find their way back into the clock

232
00:08:41,766 --> 00:08:44,400
When you can say how today differed from yesterday

233
00:08:44,466 --> 00:08:46,666
You will not be left with only the words I have been so busy lately

234
00:08:46,766 --> 00:08:48,833
Slowly you will have more specific things to say

235
00:08:49,133 --> 00:08:50,633
What did I see that day

236
00:08:50,666 --> 00:08:52,400
What did I do for the first time that day

237
00:08:52,400 --> 00:08:54,966
What did I finally stop forcing myself to do that day

238
00:08:54,966 --> 00:08:57,166
These words will not change your life at once

239
00:08:57,266 --> 00:09:00,666
But they will keep life from becoming only a compressed progress bar

240
00:09:00,800 --> 00:09:02,466
You are not missing a stretch of life

241
00:09:02,533 --> 00:09:05,000
You are missing the pins that anchor it in memory

242
00:09:05,000 --> 00:09:07,866
So next time you feel another month has vanished in a blink

243
00:09:07,900 --> 00:09:09,600
Do not rush to blame your age

244
00:09:09,600 --> 00:09:11,600
And do not rush to pack your schedule fuller

245
00:09:11,666 --> 00:09:14,233
Do one small thing this week that differs from yesterday

246
00:09:14,333 --> 00:09:16,233
Then leave one sentence before bed

247
00:09:16,333 --> 00:09:17,433
Many years from now

248
00:09:17,500 --> 00:09:19,766
You may not remember exactly what you did that day

249
00:09:19,766 --> 00:09:20,833
But you will remember

250
00:09:20,866 --> 00:09:22,133
That in those days

251
00:09:22,133 --> 00:09:23,766
You were not always rushing forward

252
00:09:23,766 --> 00:09:25,100
You also stopped sometimes

253
00:09:25,100 --> 00:09:26,366
And truly lived one day
